## 2. Why RabbitMQ for Log Transport? And What If It's Down?

### Why RabbitMQ?

| Approach | How It Works | Problem |
|---|---|---|
| ❌ Direct HTTP | Each service POSTs logs to logging-service via REST | **Synchronous** — if logging-service is slow, it slows down your payment/recharge APIs |
| ❌ Shared filesystem | All services write to same folder | Doesn't work if services run on different machines |
| ❌ Polling/scraping | Logging-service reads each service's console | Unreliable, no structured data |
| ✅ **RabbitMQ** | Each service publishes log events to a queue; logging-service consumes asynchronously | **Non-blocking, decoupled, already in your stack** |

RabbitMQ is the right choice because:
1. **Already running** in your infrastructure (no new tool to install)
2. **Async/non-blocking** — publishing a log event takes <1ms, doesn't slow down business APIs
3. **Durable queues** — if logging-service restarts, messages wait in the queue
4. **Your Saga already uses it** — proven reliable in your architecture

### What If RabbitMQ Is Down? → Local File Fallback

```mermaid
graph TD
    A["Service generates log event"] --> B{"Is RabbitMQ<br/>available?"}
    B -->|"YES (99.9% of the time)"| C["Publish to<br/>logging.events.queue"]
    C --> D["logging-service<br/>consumes & writes files"]
    B -->|"NO (RabbitMQ down)"| E["Write to local<br/>fallback file:<br/>logs/fallback-buffer.log"]
    E --> F["On RabbitMQ recovery:<br/>background thread reads<br/>fallback file and<br/>republishes to queue"]
    F --> D
```

Each service has a **dual-write strategy**:
- **Primary**: Publish `LogEvent` to RabbitMQ → logging-service writes to organized files
- **Fallback**: If RabbitMQ publish fails → write to local `logs/fallback-buffer.log`
- **Recovery**: A scheduled task in each service checks the fallback file every 30s; when RabbitMQ is back, it replays buffered events to the queue

> This means **zero log loss** even during RabbitMQ downtime.

### What About RabbitMQ's Own Logs?

We will NOT store RabbitMQ internal logs (those are managed by RabbitMQ itself at `%APPDATA%\RabbitMQ\log\`). We WILL log all **RabbitMQ-related business events** (message published, message consumed, connection errors) via the existing `log.info/error` calls — which our system will capture automatically.

